========================================================================================
 (1) The 6-fold rotation: zeta_6 = n mod (n-zeta_6), so n^6 = 1 mod Phi_6(n) (the p6/C6 action)
========================================================================================
   n   Phi_6  n^6 mod Phi6  ord(n) ord(n-1)=q mult  ord(-1)
   3       7             1       6               3        2
   4      13             1       6               3        2
   6      31             1       6               3        2
   8      57             1       6               3        2
  14     183             1       6               3        2
   9      73             1       6               3        2
  12     133             1       6               3        2
   => n has order EXACTLY 6 mod Phi_6(n): the hexagonal 6-fold rotation acts on Z/Phi_6(n) as x->n*x.
   (n-1=q is the classical Singer multiplier; -1 = n^3 is the 180-deg rotation, order 2.)

========================================================================================
 (2) Singer difference set + its 6-fold (mult-by-n) hexagonal symmetry (multiplier test)
========================================================================================
   n=3 (v=7,k=3): D=[0, 1, 3]
      mult-by-n=3 (zeta_6, 6-fold): multiplier? no
      mult-by-q=2 (Singer):       multiplier? YES shift 1
      mult-by-(-1)=n^3 (180-deg):     multiplier? no
   n=4 (v=13,k=4): D=[0, 1, 3, 9]
      mult-by-n=4 (zeta_6, 6-fold): multiplier? no
      mult-by-q=3 (Singer):       multiplier? YES shift 0
      mult-by-(-1)=n^3 (180-deg):     multiplier? no
   n=6 (v=31,k=6): D=[0, 1, 3, 8, 12, 18]
      mult-by-n=6 (zeta_6, 6-fold): multiplier? no
      mult-by-q=5 (Singer):       multiplier? YES shift 3
      mult-by-(-1)=n^3 (180-deg):     multiplier? no

========================================================================================
 (3) THE BRIDGE FRAME
========================================================================================
 Z[zeta_6] = hexagonal lattice (wallpaper p6m). Phi_6(n)=norm; Z/Phi_6(n) = hexagonal-lattice quotient.
 The Singer covering = the projective plane = the OPTIMAL covering DESIGN; carried by the p6 6-fold
 rotation (mult-by-n). Continuous side: the hexagonal lattice is the THINNEST covering of the plane
 (Kershner 1939) -- the unique optimal LATTICE covering, p6m-symmetric. So the bridge:
   discrete design-optimality (projective plane / Steiner)  <-->  continuous covering-optimality
   (hexagonal lattice, Kershner),  TRANSFERRED by the shared p6/Eisenstein symmetry.
 The covering-min n/Phi_6(n) is the density of the hexagonal-symmetric (p6) optimal covering.
